1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,759
At 99.9999% of light speed,

2
00:00:04,759 --> 00:00:10,052
the whole sky collapses into one patch
of light ahead. You age one year.

3
00:00:10,052 --> 00:00:12,321
Earth ages 707.

4
00:00:12,721 --> 00:00:15,578
One speed on the way here
kills you. Which one?

5
00:00:16,878 --> 00:00:21,948
Here's the trip. You get a ship with the
mass of the International Space Station,

6
00:00:21,948 --> 00:00:24,990
about 420 tonnes, and one control:

7
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:27,694
a dial that adds nines to your speed.

8
00:00:28,094 --> 00:00:33,034
10%. 50%. 90%. Then 99, 99.9.

9
00:00:33,034 --> 00:00:35,280
One nine at a time.

10
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:37,835
At every stop, we check four things.

11
00:00:37,835 --> 00:00:39,067
Your clock against Earth's.

12
00:00:39,067 --> 00:00:40,914
What you see out the window.

13
00:00:40,914 --> 00:00:43,993
What's hitting the front
of the ship. And the bill.

14
00:00:44,393 --> 00:00:47,050
And this sky is not a painting.

15
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:51,225
It's tens of thousands of real
stars from a star catalog,

16
00:00:51,225 --> 00:00:55,779
moved and recolored by the actual
equations of relativity, frame by frame.

17
00:00:56,179 --> 00:00:58,120
One number runs the whole journey.

18
00:00:58,120 --> 00:00:59,413
Physicists call it gamma.

19
00:00:59,413 --> 00:01:03,941
It says how many seconds pass on
Earth for every second on your clock,

20
00:01:03,941 --> 00:01:06,852
and it lives in the corner of your screen.

21
00:01:08,152 --> 00:01:13,192
First stop. 10%. You're covering
30,000 kilometers every second.

22
00:01:13,592 --> 00:01:17,748
That's 156 times faster
than the fastest thing

23
00:01:17,748 --> 00:01:20,770
humans have ever built,
NASA's Parker Solar Probe.

24
00:01:21,170 --> 00:01:23,235
And yet your clock barely notices.

25
00:01:23,235 --> 00:01:27,708
For every year you spend on board,
Earth gets about 2 extra days.

26
00:01:28,108 --> 00:01:32,010
Out the window, the stars have
crept a few degrees forward.

27
00:01:32,010 --> 00:01:34,139
Ahead, they look a touch bluer.

28
00:01:34,139 --> 00:01:35,558
Behind, a touch redder.

29
00:01:35,558 --> 00:01:36,622
Nothing dramatic yet.

30
00:01:37,022 --> 00:01:38,509
So what's hitting you?

31
00:01:38,509 --> 00:01:39,624
Space isn't empty.

32
00:01:39,624 --> 00:01:42,970
There's about 1 hydrogen atom
in every cubic centimeter,

33
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:45,572
and you're plowing into all of them.

34
00:01:45,972 --> 00:01:49,536
Each one arrives with 4.7
million electron volts,

35
00:01:49,536 --> 00:01:53,099
and stops in less than
half a millimeter of water.

36
00:01:53,099 --> 00:01:54,881
Your hull shrugs it off.

37
00:01:55,281 --> 00:01:59,332
The bill. Getting this ship
to 10% takes as much energy

38
00:01:59,332 --> 00:02:03,046
as the entire world uses
in about 3.9 months.

39
00:02:03,446 --> 00:02:08,344
And Alpha Centauri, the nearest
star system, is 43.7 years away.

40
00:02:08,344 --> 00:02:11,735
You'd step off the ship as an old person.

41
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:13,242
Keep that in mind.

42
00:02:13,242 --> 00:02:16,256
The destinations are
about to get much closer.

43
00:02:17,556 --> 00:02:19,024
Turn the dial. 50%.

44
00:02:19,024 --> 00:02:20,493
Half the speed of light.

45
00:02:20,893 --> 00:02:22,823
Now the clocks really split.

46
00:02:22,823 --> 00:02:27,068
For every year you live, Earth lives 1.15.

47
00:02:27,068 --> 00:02:29,384
That's 57 extra days, every year.

48
00:02:29,784 --> 00:02:32,866
Look at a star that was right beside you.

49
00:02:32,866 --> 00:02:34,236
It isn't there anymore.

50
00:02:34,236 --> 00:02:38,688
It has slid forward, and now appears
just 60 degrees from straight ahead.

51
00:02:38,688 --> 00:02:41,428
The whole sky is sliding toward your nose.

52
00:02:41,828 --> 00:02:46,473
Ahead, a star like our Sun now
glows at 10,000 kelvin, blue-white.

53
00:02:46,473 --> 00:02:48,974
Behind you, the same kind of star

54
00:02:48,974 --> 00:02:52,904
looks like a dim red ember, at 3,300.

55
00:02:53,304 --> 00:02:59,440
And the hydrogen? Each atom now
carries 145 million electron volts.

56
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:04,262
That's the same energy hospitals
use to fire protons into tumors.

57
00:03:04,662 --> 00:03:07,224
Those reach deep inside a human body.

58
00:03:07,224 --> 00:03:11,981
But a tank of water, less than 16
centimeters thick, stops them cold.

59
00:03:11,981 --> 00:03:15,275
Line the bow with it.
Problem solved. For now.

60
00:03:15,675 --> 00:03:20,633
The bill jumps to about 9.9 years
of everything humanity uses.

61
00:03:20,633 --> 00:03:24,447
And Alpha Centauri is now 7.57 years

62
00:03:24,447 --> 00:03:28,261
away on your clock, 8.74 on Earth's.

63
00:03:29,561 --> 00:03:32,582
90%. This is the one I warned you about.

64
00:03:32,982 --> 00:03:37,711
Each hydrogen atom now hits with
1.21 billion electron volts.

65
00:03:37,711 --> 00:03:40,258
That's more energy than its own mass.

66
00:03:40,258 --> 00:03:43,168
And these don't stop in a water tank.

67
00:03:43,568 --> 00:03:46,927
They cross about 83.3
centimeters of water,

68
00:03:46,927 --> 00:03:49,912
on average, before they
even hit a nucleus.

69
00:03:49,912 --> 00:03:53,644
Then they shatter it into
a spray of new particles.

70
00:03:53,644 --> 00:03:57,376
A thin shield doesn't stop
the beam. It multiplies it.

71
00:03:57,776 --> 00:04:00,443
Unshielded, that beam
would give you a lethal

72
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:03,110
dose of radiation in under half a second.

73
00:04:03,510 --> 00:04:06,602
So what kills you isn't
a planet, or a wall.

74
00:04:06,602 --> 00:04:08,767
It's the emptiest place in the universe.

75
00:04:08,767 --> 00:04:13,096
One atom in a space the size of
a sugar cube, hit hard enough.

76
00:04:13,496 --> 00:04:17,108
From here on, you need meters
of shielding in front of you,

77
00:04:17,108 --> 00:04:18,914
and we'll assume you have it.

78
00:04:18,914 --> 00:04:21,623
Because the view is about
to get very strange.

79
00:04:22,023 --> 00:04:25,105
A star that sits right beside you now

80
00:04:25,105 --> 00:04:27,801
appears just 26 degrees
from straight ahead.

81
00:04:27,801 --> 00:04:32,809
Half of the entire sky is squeezed
into a circle 52 degrees wide.

82
00:04:33,209 --> 00:04:35,801
And behind you, the sky goes black.

83
00:04:35,801 --> 00:04:39,875
Sunlike stars there are
shifted to 1,300 kelvin,

84
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:43,208
deep into the infrared,
where your eyes can't follow.

85
00:04:43,608 --> 00:04:46,069
The bill: 82 years of world energy.

86
00:04:46,069 --> 00:04:50,992
But Alpha Centauri is now
just 2.12 years away for you.

87
00:04:52,292 --> 00:04:54,406
Add a nine. 99%.

88
00:04:54,806 --> 00:04:57,633
Spend one year out here, then come home,

89
00:04:57,633 --> 00:05:00,460
and your friends are
about seven years older.

90
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:03,994
Every year aboard is 7.09 on Earth.

91
00:05:04,394 --> 00:05:10,279
Out the window, half of the universe
now fits in a circle 16 degrees across.

92
00:05:10,279 --> 00:05:13,026
About 31 full Moons, side by side.

93
00:05:13,426 --> 00:05:16,266
And every square meter of your bow absorbs

94
00:05:16,266 --> 00:05:19,815
272 kilowatts from the gas you're hitting.

95
00:05:19,815 --> 00:05:23,365
About 200 times the sunlight
that warms the Earth.

96
00:05:23,765 --> 00:05:26,523
Alpha Centauri? 7.5 months away.

97
00:05:26,523 --> 00:05:32,040
The center of the galaxy is
still 3,700 years. Not yet.

98
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:39,188
99.9%. You'd think the stars ahead
would only get brighter. They don't.

99
00:05:39,588 --> 00:05:44,124
A Sun-like star dead ahead
now looks 260,000 kelvin.

100
00:05:44,124 --> 00:05:47,614
Most of its light has been
pushed into the ultraviolet,

101
00:05:47,614 --> 00:05:50,754
past what your eyes can see, so it fades.

102
00:05:51,154 --> 00:05:55,586
Each year aboard is now
22.4 years on Earth.

103
00:05:55,586 --> 00:05:59,213
And Alpha Centauri is 71.4 days away.

104
00:05:59,613 --> 00:06:02,736
And here's the bill for
that one extra nine.

105
00:06:02,736 --> 00:06:06,900
About 1,400 years of all
the energy humanity uses.

106
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:10,370
Every nine costs more than
all the nines before it.

107
00:06:11,670 --> 00:06:15,391
99.99%. Now look straight ahead.

108
00:06:15,791 --> 00:06:18,311
Science fiction promised a starbow here:

109
00:06:18,311 --> 00:06:21,672
a rainbow ring of stars around your nose.

110
00:06:21,672 --> 00:06:27,552
But in 1979, two physicists ran the
numbers properly, and the ring vanished.

111
00:06:27,552 --> 00:06:28,812
This simulation agrees.

112
00:06:28,812 --> 00:06:33,853
Every star piles up dead ahead,
bluest and densest at the center.

113
00:06:34,253 --> 00:06:36,126
And something else lights up.

114
00:06:36,126 --> 00:06:38,748
The faint afterglow of the Big Bang,

115
00:06:38,748 --> 00:06:41,370
normally 2.725 kelvin,

116
00:06:41,370 --> 00:06:45,115
is heated dead ahead to 385 kelvin.

117
00:06:45,115 --> 00:06:46,614
Hotter than boiling water.

118
00:06:46,614 --> 00:06:49,610
The darkness in front of you is warm.

119
00:06:50,010 --> 00:06:53,258
A single speck of dust,
one millionth of a gram,

120
00:06:53,258 --> 00:06:56,830
would now hit you like 1.5 tonnes of TNT.

121
00:06:57,230 --> 00:07:00,984
Each year aboard is 70.7 on Earth.

122
00:07:00,984 --> 00:07:04,320
Alpha Centauri is 22.6 days away.

123
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:10,159
The center of the galaxy, 368
years. Closer. Not close enough.

124
00:07:11,459 --> 00:07:15,338
Let's skip a nine. 99.9999%.

125
00:07:15,338 --> 00:07:18,864
This is the sky from the
beginning of the video.

126
00:07:19,264 --> 00:07:22,167
Half of the universe fits inside a circle

127
00:07:22,167 --> 00:07:26,158
smaller than the Moon,
just 0.16 degrees wide.

128
00:07:26,158 --> 00:07:27,609
And at its center,

129
00:07:27,609 --> 00:07:32,689
the Big Bang's afterglow is
blueshifted to 3,850 kelvin.

130
00:07:32,689 --> 00:07:35,954
Hot enough to glow orange,
right where you're heading.

131
00:07:36,354 --> 00:07:39,884
Your clock: one year. Earth's: 707.

132
00:07:40,284 --> 00:07:45,916
And one hydrogen atom now hits
with 663 billion electron volts.

133
00:07:45,916 --> 00:07:49,939
CERN compares that kind of
energy to a flying mosquito.

134
00:07:49,939 --> 00:07:53,961
You're taking two-thirds of a
mosquito, packed into one proton,

135
00:07:53,961 --> 00:07:57,582
trillions of times a second,
on every square meter.

136
00:07:57,982 --> 00:08:01,961
Your bow is soaking up 32
megawatts on every square meter.

137
00:08:01,961 --> 00:08:06,302
And a speck of dust now
hits like 15 tonnes of TNT.

138
00:08:06,702 --> 00:08:10,807
The bill: about 45,000 years
of humanity's total energy,

139
00:08:10,807 --> 00:08:13,270
for one ship, at one speed.

140
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:16,159
But here's what you get for it.

141
00:08:16,159 --> 00:08:19,715
The center of the Milky Way,
26,000 light-years away,

142
00:08:19,715 --> 00:08:23,271
is now 36.8 years away on your clock.

143
00:08:23,271 --> 00:08:25,760
You could get there in one lifetime.

144
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:31,099
When you arrive, Earth's calendar
has moved on by about 26,000 years.

145
00:08:31,099 --> 00:08:32,238
Remember that number.

146
00:08:32,238 --> 00:08:34,138
It's about to look small.

147
00:08:35,438 --> 00:08:38,174
Real ships can't just jump to a speed.

148
00:08:38,174 --> 00:08:41,595
So for the last trip,
picture an engine that pushes

149
00:08:41,595 --> 00:08:44,673
at exactly 1 g, Earth's
gravity, the whole way.

150
00:08:44,673 --> 00:08:46,725
On board, you'd feel at home.

151
00:08:47,125 --> 00:08:51,412
Speed up for half the trip, flip
around, brake for the other half.

152
00:08:51,412 --> 00:08:56,029
On that ship, the center of
the galaxy is 19.8 years away.

153
00:08:56,429 --> 00:09:00,511
And the Andromeda Galaxy,
2.5 million light-years away,

154
00:09:00,511 --> 00:09:04,184
takes just 28.6 years on your clock.

155
00:09:04,584 --> 00:09:07,236
At the halfway point, you'd be moving

156
00:09:07,236 --> 00:09:12,919
at 99.99999999997%

157
00:09:12,919 --> 00:09:15,570
of the speed of light. 12 nines.

158
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:18,980
Faster than any proton in
the Large Hadron Collider.

159
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:21,381
Could anyone build that engine?

160
00:09:21,381 --> 00:09:24,583
Not with anything we know how to make.

161
00:09:24,583 --> 00:09:29,386
The energy alone is about 82 million
years of humanity's total supply.

162
00:09:29,386 --> 00:09:31,787
But relativity itself doesn't say no.

163
00:09:33,087 --> 00:09:36,155
So here's your life. You leave at 25.

164
00:09:36,155 --> 00:09:39,607
You fly to Andromeda,
turn around, and fly home.

165
00:09:39,607 --> 00:09:41,524
On your clock, 57 years.

166
00:09:41,524 --> 00:09:43,442
You step out at 82.

167
00:09:43,842 --> 00:09:46,212
On Earth, 5 million years have passed.

168
00:09:46,612 --> 00:09:49,628
For scale: the oldest
fossil of our own genus,

169
00:09:49,628 --> 00:09:52,644
Homo, is about 2.8 million years old.

170
00:09:52,644 --> 00:09:57,671
You'd come home further into the future
than the first humans are in the past.

171
00:09:58,071 --> 00:10:00,773
You could cross to another
galaxy in one lifetime.

172
00:10:00,773 --> 00:10:03,776
You just can't come back
to the world you left.

173
00:10:04,176 --> 00:10:08,085
If you'd rather fall than fly,
the next trip goes straight down.

174
00:10:08,085 --> 00:10:10,039
What If You Fell Into Jupiter?

175
00:10:10,039 --> 00:10:11,668
This was The If Engine.

176
00:10:11,668 --> 00:10:13,622
Run the impossible. Do the math.
